# clal-compiled-artefact — writing a .lap, and what VLISP-COMPILE should do

STATUS: DONE in 2.0.10. Filed 2026-08-27 while implementing 2.0.9;
answered by pjb 2026-08-28 and implemented the same day.

## What exists after 2.0.9

CLAL-COMPILE-FILE and CLAL-COMPILE-SYSTEM compile into the RUNNING
IMAGE. That is genuinely useful — it is what makes a compilation unit,
and therefore deferred forward references across files, possible — but
nothing is written to disk. CLAL-COMPILE-SYSTEM's OUTPUT-FILE argument
is therefore required to be nil and SIGNALS otherwise, rather than
being accepted and quietly ignored.

## What is in the way

The transpiler emits code that quotes AUTOLISP-SYMBOL (and
AUTOLISP-STRING) structs as literals. COMPILE accepts that — the
literal is the very object that already exists in the image. COMPILE-FILE
does not: it must externalize every literal, and a struct with no
MAKE-LOAD-FORM method cannot be externalized.

The fix itself is small and is already named in compiler.issue:

    (defmethod make-load-form ((object autolisp-symbol) &optional env)
      (declare (ignore env))
      `(intern-autolisp-symbol ,(autolisp-symbol-name object)))

Re-interning by name is the right externalization and not merely the
easy one: a symbol IS its name in this engine, and reconstructing it
through INTERN-AUTOLISP-SYMBOL puts the loaded artefact's symbols in
the loading image's table, which is the only place they could usefully
live. A structure-copying MAKE-LOAD-FORM would produce symbols EQ to
nothing, and every EQ test in the runtime would quietly stop matching.

## The part that needs deciding, not just coding

What IS a .lap? The extension is already claimed and documented
(CLAL-MODULE-EXTENSION "compiled-app" => ".lap", "the clautolisp
analogue of AutoCAD .vlx / BricsCAD .des"). But an artefact format is a
compatibility promise, and these questions have to be answered before
one is written rather than after:

  - Is it a host FASL under another name? Then it is SBCL-version-
    specific and CCL cannot load it — clautolisp supports both. A .lap
    that silently fails to load on the other host is worse than none.
  - Does it contain the transpiled Common Lisp, or the AutoLISP source,
    or both? BricsCAD's .des is encrypted source; AutoCAD's .fas is
    compiled. clautolisp does not have to copy either, but it has to
    CHOOSE, because LOAD must recognise the file and do the right thing.
  - What happens on version skew — a .lap built by 2.0.9 loaded by a
    later release? A version stamp in the header and a refusal is the
    minimum; silently loading a stale artefact is the failure mode that
    wastes days.

## Then, and only then, VLISP-COMPILE

VLISP-COMPILE is currently a documented no-op returning nil, and that is
HONEST: it is specified to return T when the output file was created,
and clautolisp creates none. It should not be made to return T for an
in-memory compilation — that would claim a file exists.

Once .lap exists, VLISP-COMPILE becomes the vendor-API spelling of
CLAL-COMPILE-FILE: it takes 'st / 'lsm / 'lsa (which BricsCAD parses and
ignores, and which clautolisp can map onto SPEED levels — 'st => the
per-file unit, 'lsm / 'lsa => the system-wide one, which is what those
mode names mean in the VLISP IDE), writes a .lap next to the source, and
returns T.

NOTE ON THE NAME: pjb referred to it as "vlisp-compile-file". The
specified name is VLISP-COMPILE (spec ch. "Function Entry:
VLISP-COMPILE"); there is no VLISP-COMPILE-FILE in either vendor.


## RESOLVED — what pjb decided, 2026-08-28

The design question above ("what IS a .lap?") was answered, and the
answer was to stop trying to be clever:

  - An artefact CANNOT be a Lisp image. AutoCAD and BricsCAD load
    applications into a document that already has others loaded, in any
    order. Partial images would be the only other way, and no current
    Common Lisp produces them (one used to).
  - So a .lap is a native host FASL. Nothing else can be loaded into a
    running image.
  - Cross-host portability is therefore GIVEN UP, deliberately: a .lap
    built by an SBCL clautolisp does not load in a CCL one. A portable
    format means a custom byte-code FASL, and bocl's experience with one
    was not good on speed. (CLISP could do it — .fas is byte code for a
    VM independent of the host, so a Windows-compiled .fas loads on
    macOS, as long as CFFI is not involved. That is a property of a
    byte-code implementation, not something SBCL or CCL can be talked
    into.)
  - Cross-VERSION compatibility is given up too, for now. In principle
    only a M. or M.m difference should break a .lap and a .d difference
    should not; until things stabilize, no compatibility is promised.
  - And the NAME stays simple: just .lap, not
    name-sbcl-2.0.10-x86_64.lap. Builds that differ live in different
    directories, exactly as the binary releases already do. (CCL's own
    extensions — .lx86fas, .dx64fas … — are precisely the naming scheme
    being declined.)

## What was implemented

  - EXTERNALIZATION, and it is not MAKE-LOAD-FORM after all. The
    artefact is generated as SOURCE and COMPILE-FILE'd, because
    COMPILE-FILE is the only door to a FASL — so the problem is not
    externalizing a literal but PRINTING one. AUTOLISP-SYMBOL has a
    PRINT-OBJECT method that writes its bare name, so printing one into
    a source file yields a token that reads back as a COMMON LISP
    symbol: nothing signals, the artefact is just wrong. Hence
    EXTERNALIZE-DATUM, which emits CONSTRUCTOR forms —
    (INTERN-AUTOLISP-SYMBOL "FOO") — landing the symbol in the LOADING
    image's table, which was the point of re-interning by name all
    along.
  - LOAD-TIME-VALUE around every constructed literal. Without it a
    fallback inside a loop — a plain (AUTOLISP-EVAL '<form> context) —
    would rebuild its form on every iteration. Not merely slower: source
    positions are recorded against body conses, so a form rebuilt per
    call is a form the debugger can no longer locate.
  - A .lap keeps each function's SOURCE BODY beside its compiled one.
    Dropping it would load faster and make every function from an
    artefact impossible to instrument, since the debugger weaves its
    fork FROM the body.
  - CLAL-COMPILE-SYSTEM emits all its sources into ONE generated file,
    so one COMPILE-FILE covers the system. That is what makes it a
    system rather than a loop, and it is a better answer than the
    WITH-COMPILATION-UNIT sketch: a real single compilation, not merely
    a shared warning context.
  - VLISP-COMPILE is now the vendor-API spelling of CLAL-COMPILE-FILE.

## Still open, deliberately

A host-independent .lap. Noted as a nice feature and deferred; see the
bocl experience above. If it is ever revisited, the CLISP property is
the existence proof that it can be done, and the reason it costs speed.
